🛡️ Stay Safe Online Checklist for Indian Students
1.	[ ] Never Enter UPI PIN to Receive Money: Scammers often send "collect requests" (payment requests) disguised as refunds or job payments. Remember: You only enter your UPI PIN to send money, never to receive it.
2.	[ ] Verify Beneficiary Name on UPI Page: Before clicking "Pay" and entering your MPIN, stop and read the payee's name displayed on the screen to ensure it matches the person you intend to pay, not just the phone number.
3.	[ ] Guard Your UPI PIN/MPIN and OTPs: Never share your MPIN, card CVV, or OTPs with anyone, even those claiming to be "bank officials," "customer care," or "Army personnel." Legitimate entities never ask for this.
4.	[ ] Avoid Screen-Sharing Apps (AnyDesk/TeamViewer): Do not install remote access apps if requested by "bank support" to resolve a transaction issue. These apps allow scammers to view your screen and steal your banking PINs.
5.	[ ] Use Unique Passwords + 2FA: Use a password manager to generate unique, complex passwords for all accounts (Instagram, LinkedIn, Gmail, Netbanking). Enable Two-Factor Authentication (2FA) for all accounts.
6.	[ ] Be Wary of "Easy Money" & Fake Job Offers: Phishing scams often target students with "part-time job," "data entry," or "high return investment" offers. Verify the company independently; do not pay any "registration fee".
7.	[ ] Use a VPN on Public Wi-Fi: Avoid accessing banking apps or shopping on hostel or cafe public Wi-Fi. If unavoidable, use a trusted VPN to encrypt your data.
8.	[ ] Set Lower UPI Transaction Limits: Adjust your UPI app settings to a lower daily transaction limit. This ensures that even if your account is compromised, the potential financial damage is minimized.
🚨 How to Report Cyber Crime in India
If you are a victim of financial fraud, early reporting is crucial for blocking funds.
·	Helpline: Call 1930 (National Cyber Crime Helpline) immediately.
·	Online Portal: Report incidents at cybercrime.gov.in.
